the anchors a view hands out

NSLayoutAnchor was an empty class and the axis subclasses did not exist, so -[NSView topAnchor]
raised and took iA Writer with its window otherwise built. An anchor is a pair, the item and
which attribute of it, and every method makes an NSLayoutConstraint out of that pair, which is
all an anchor does on macOS either. There is no solver behind it, exactly as for the
constraints themselves, so a caller gets a real object recording the relation it asked for
while the layout still comes from the autoresizing masks.

The names live in Foundation because NSLayoutConstraint does: a second class of the same name
in AppKit is a coin toss the runtime resolves however it likes.

